      <span>EPA Section 608 &amp; ASHRAE Standard 15</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-gray-900 tracking-tight mb-3">
      Why You Must Pull a 500-Micron Vacuum on Mini-Split Linesets
    </h1>
    <p class="text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-6">
      When amateur DIYers install a mini-split, many simply run the vacuum pump for 15 minutes, look at their analog gauge needle pointing near -30 inHg, and release the refrigerant. Within two seasons, the compressor seizes. Here is the thermodynamic science behind why pulling a verified <strong>500-micron deep vacuum</strong> with a digital micron gauge is non-negotiable.
    </p>

    <!-- Quick Stats Grid -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 pt-6 border-t border-gray-100">
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Target Deep Vacuum</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-emerald-600 mt-0.5">&le; 500 Microns (0.5 Torr)</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Water Boiling Point</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900 mt-0.5">Boils at 50°F at 4,500 Microns</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Decay Test Rule</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900 mt-0.5">Hold &lt; 1,000 Microns for 10m</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Main Editorial Content -->
  <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6 sm:p-8 mb-8 prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">Thermodynamics: Boiling Water at Room Temperature</h2>
    <p>
      At sea-level atmospheric pressure (14.7 PSI or 760,000 microns), water boils into steam at 212°F (100°C). But as pressure inside an enclosed copper pipe drops, the boiling point of water drops rapidly:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>At <strong>100,000 microns</strong>, water boils at 125°F.</li>
      <li>At <strong>10,000 microns</strong>, water boils at 52°F.</li>
      <li>At <strong>4,500 microns</strong>, water boils at 32°F (freezing).</li>
      <li>At <strong>500 microns</strong>, water boils at -12°F (-24°C)!</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      By pulling the lineset pressure down to <strong>500 microns</strong>, any microscopic moisture droplets trapped inside the copper boil violently into gas at normal room temperature. The vacuum pump pulls the gaseous vapor out of the copper, leaving the line 100% bone dry.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">The Hydrofluoric Acid Compressor Death Sentence</h2>
    <p>
      Modern mini-splits use synthetic <strong>POE (polyolester) lubricating oil</strong> for the high-speed scroll compressor. POE oil is extremely hygroscopic—it absorbs water molecules out of air within seconds.
    </p>
    <p>
      If moisture remains trapped inside the copper lineset when refrigerant (R410A or R32) is released:
    </p>
    <div class="bg-red-50 p-5 rounded-xl border border-red-200 my-4 not-prose">
      <div class="font-mono text-sm text-red-900 font-bold mb-2">Moisture + Heat + Refrigerant = Lethal Acid</div>
      <p class="text-xs text-red-800 leading-relaxed mb-0">
        Under high operating discharge temperatures (180°F+), water chemically reacts with fluorinated refrigerant to produce <strong>hydrofluoric acid (HF)</strong> and hydrochloric acid. This acid strips the thin enamel coating off the copper electric motor windings, causing a catastrophic internal electrical short and seizing the compressor bearings within 24 to 36 months!
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">How to Perform a 10-Minute Vacuum Decay Test</h2>
    <p>
      Pulling down to 500 microns is only half the job. You must prove the system is both <strong>leak-free</strong> and <strong>dehydrated</strong>:
    </p>
    <ol class="space-y-3 not-prose my-4">
      <li class="p-3 bg-gray-50 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 text-xs text-gray-700">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">1. Blank Off the Pump</strong>
        Once your gauge reads 300 to 500 microns, close the valve on your core removal tool or gauge manifold to isolate the lineset from the vacuum pump. Turn off the pump.
      </li>
      <li class="p-3 bg-gray-50 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 text-xs text-gray-700">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">2. Observe the Micron Rise Over 10 Minutes</strong>
        The micron reading will naturally rise slightly (called out-gassing) and should level off.
        <ul class="mt-2 space-y-1 pl-4 list-disc text-gray-600">
          <li><strong>PASS:</strong> Pressure rises to 600–800 microns and stays flat for 10 minutes. The system is dry and tight. Release refrigerant!</li>
          <li><strong>MOISTURE FAILURE:</strong> Pressure rises steadily past 1,000 microns, levels off around 2,500–3,500 microns, and stalls. Water is still boiling inside the pipe. Continue pumping.</li>
          <li><strong>LEAK FAILURE:</strong> Pressure rises continuously straight back to atmospheric pressure (760,000 microns). You have a mechanical flare leak.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <!-- Jobsite Field Note -->
  <FieldNote
    author="Marcus Vance"
    role="Master Systems & MEP Building Specialist"
    experience="22+ Years Field Experience"
    avatarBg="bg-amber-100 text-amber-800"
    initials="MV"
    title="Why Analog Manifold Gauges are Legally Useless Here"
    note="A standard analog blue manifold gauge dial has hash marks from 0 down to -30 inHg. Between -29 inHg and -30 inHg lies a massive pressure gap of 25,400 microns! Looking at an analog needle pinned at -30 inHg tells you absolutely nothing about whether your lineset is at 25,000 microns (wet and saturated with air) or 500 microns (dry and deep-evacuated). Analog gauges are designed for reading 300 PSI refrigerant pressure, not measuring microscopic vacuum. You must invest $110 in a digital electronic micron gauge (like a Fieldpiece MG44 or Yellow Jacket). It plugs directly into the service port and displays exact numerical micron counts down to single digits."
  />

  <!-- Costly Mistakes -->
  <CostlyMistakes
    tradeName="Mini-Split Evacuation"
    mistakes={[
      {
        number: 1,
        title: "Connecting Micron Gauge to the Vacuum Pump Instead of the Lineset",
        costImpact: "$650 (False Reading from Pump Oil)",
        description: "Plugging your micron gauge into the vacuum pump port reads the pump's internal vacuum, not the actual conditions inside the 25-foot copper lineset.",
        solution: "Connect the digital micron gauge to a valve core removal tool directly on the condenser service port so it measures the true lineset environment."
      },
      {
        number: 2,
        title: "Using Worn-Out, Water-Saturated Vacuum Pump Oil",
        costImpact: "$450 (Stalled Vacuum at 1,500 Microns)",
        description: "Vacuum pump oil absorbs moisture from every job. Saturated, cloudy oil cannot pull below 1,500 microns, burning up pump vanes.",
        solution: "Change vacuum pump oil whenever it turns cloudy or every 3 installations using clean, clear high-vacuum mineral oil."
      },
      {
        number: 3,
        title: "Cracking Service Valves Before Completing the Decay Test",
        costImpact: "$1,800 (Contaminating Pre-Charged Refrigerant)",
        description: "Releasing factory R410A refrigerant while a leak still exists dumps the entire charge into the atmosphere, requiring an expensive EPA recovery and recharge.",
        solution: "Never touch the hex key service valves until the 10-minute micron decay test has proven the system holds below 1,000 microns without moving."
      }
    ]}
  />

